tal": 5.45, "baseline_margin": 6.54, "analyst_adjustment": 0.96, "key_factors": ["Consistent polling lead for Sand averaging about 8-9 points across 15 surveys", "Open seat with no incumbent on the ballot in an R-leaning state", "Expert ratings uniformly favor a Democratic flip, from Tilt D to Solid D", "Prediction markets price Sand near 87-89% to win"], "rationale": "Polling has been remarkably consistent since early September, with even Republican-sponsored pollsters showing Sand ahead, and every major rater calls the race a Democratic lean or better. The state's Republican lean and the volatility of open-seat races argue for trimming the polling average slightly rather than taking it at face value.", "watch": "A late debate-driven shift toward Lahn or polling convergence toward the state's Republican baseline could narrow the race considerably.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Kim Reynolds (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Iowa's governorship is open this year with Kim Reynolds retiring, and State Auditor Rob Sand has built a steady lead over Republican Zach Lahn. Polls since early September consistently show Sand ahead, with recent surveys from Emerson, SSRS, Suffolk, and the New York Times/Siena all giving him margins of four to twelve points. Even Republican-aligned pollsters show him in front, though by narrower margins.\n\nExpert raters uniformly expect a Democratic flip, with most calling the race Lean D and the Silver Bulletin going further. Prediction markets price Sand's chances near 87 to 89 percent. The main caution is that Iowa remains a Republican-leaning state, so a late shift or debate-driven momentum for Lahn could tighten what polls currently show as a comfortable Sand advantage.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.8753, "margin": 5.7}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.8859, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.8669, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.8657, "margin": 6.0}, {"date":